WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.762
Run the project query once so you can see the final columns.

00:00:03.762 --> 00:00:07.207
The query uses several common table expressions.

00:00:07.207 --> 00:00:11.772
The first stage normalizes name, email, and phone values.

00:00:11.772 --> 00:00:14.317
The second parses supported dates.

00:00:14.317 --> 00:00:20.667
The third ranks duplicates. The final SELECT creates clean output fields and a row status.

00:00:20.667 --> 00:00:23.557
We will unpack those stages separately.

00:00:23.557 --> 00:00:30.317
Connect this idea to a healthcare pipeline validating contact, demographic, and event fields.

00:00:30.317 --> 00:00:35.932
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:35.932 --> 00:00:43.242
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:43.242 --> 00:00:48.987
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:48.987 --> 00:00:54.821
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
